[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Tasks don't exist yet. How should that be handled? → Notifications defines it[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the substantive one. With one person in one browser, every change is self-inflicted — you edited the task, so
  telling you "this task changed" is noise. That makes the trigger question the real design decision, not the plumbing.

  [1mThe fork:[0m which events actually earn a notification.

  - [1mDue-date reminders only[0m — "due in 1 hour", "overdue". The only trigger that tells you something you didn't already
    know. Needs a clock/scheduler and a per-task due date; cheap to extend later.
  - [1mReminders + activity feed of all changes[0m — every create/edit/complete lands in a persistent bell-menu feed with
    read/unread. More machinery (event log, storage, unread state), and most entries tell you what you just did. Its
    real payoff is being the shape you'd keep if a backend and other users arrive — so it's the expensive-to-add-later
    option.
  - [1mReminders + toasts on your own actions[0m — toasts for "task completed / undo" are useful, but they're UI feedback, not
    notifications; they'd live in the task UI and not in the notification subsystem at all.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m due-date reminders as the notification system, with toasts treated as ordinary task-UI feedback.
  It's the only part that carries real information today, and it keeps the subsystem small enough to be worth its own
  module. I'd pick the activity feed only if you know multi-user is coming — building an event log for an audience of
  one is the classic YAGNI trap.
